          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_tropical_geometry"} Relation to tropical geometry.
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#CompleteProofs"} Complete proofs
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#computation_via_topological_recursion"} Computation via topological recursion
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            To every complex 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+variety"} Calabi-Yau variety
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are associated two similar but differing types of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "-supersymmetric" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFTs"} 2d CFTs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \hat X
            which exchanges the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+numbers"} Hodge numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{1,1}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{1,2}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SCFT
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SCFT_A(X)
            is expected to be equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi SCFT
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SCFT_B(\hat X)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SCFT
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi SCFT
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  SCFT_A(X) \simeq SCFT_B(\hat X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em mirror symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            (
            %a{:href => "#StromingerYauZaslow96"}> Strominger-Yau-Zaslow 96
            ).
          %p
            In this form mirror symmetry remains a conjecture, not the least because for the moment there is no complete construction of these SCFTs. But to every such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SCFT
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SCFT(X)
            one can associate two
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(X)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=1
            supersymmetric field theories were obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
            using a “topological twist”. The topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            can be expressed in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            of a variety and the topological B-model can be expressed in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            of a variety.
          %p
            These topological theories are easier to understand and do retain a little bit of the information encoded in the full SCFTs. In terms of these the statement of mirror symmetry says that passing to mirror CYs
            %em exchanges
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            with the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A(X) \simeq B(\hat X),\,\,\,\,\,\,\,B(X)\simeq A(\hat X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            By a version of the
            = succeed "-theorem," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} A-∞ categories
            that are
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+categories"} Calabi-Yau categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} Fukaya category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fuk
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fuk(X)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which can be understood as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            representing the Lagrangian intersection theory on the underlying
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} enhancement
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^b_\infty(\hat X)
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat X
          %p
            In terms of this data, mirror symmetry is the assertion that these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} A-∞ categories
            are equivalent and simultaneously the same under exchange
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↔
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\leftrightarrow \hat{X}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fuk
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi and
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Fuk
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Fuk(X) \simeq D^b_\infty(\hat{X}),
                  \\,\,\,\, and \,\,\,\,
                  Fuk(\hat{X}) \simeq D^b_\infty(X).
          %p
            This categorical formulation was introduced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            in 1994 under the name
            = succeed "." do
              %strong homological mirror symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            and independently by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"} Kevin Costello
            = succeed "(see" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+category"} Calabi-Yau A-∞-category
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
          %p
            The mirror symmetry conjecture roughly claims that every Calabi-Yau 3-fold has a mirror. In fact one considers (mirror symmetry for) degenerating families for Calabi-Yau 3-folds in large volume limit (which may be expressed precisely via the Gromov-Hausdorff metric). The appropriate definition of (an appropriate version of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} Fukaya category
            of a symplectic manifold is difficult to achieve in desired generality. Invariants/tools of Fukaya category include symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Floer+homology"} Floer homology
            and Gromov-Witten invariants (building up the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+cohomology"} quantum cohomology
          %p
            Mirror symmetry is related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            on each fiber of an associated Lagrangian fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#StromingerYauZaslow96"} Strominger-Yau-Zaslow 96
          %p
            Although the non-Calabi-Yau case may be of lesser interest to physics, one can still formulate some mirror symmetry statements for, for instance, Fano manifolds. The mirror to a Fano manifold is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
            (see
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#HoriVafa00"} Hori-Vafa 00
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            of the Fano (given by the Fukaya category) is equivalent to the B-model of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
            (given by the category of matrix factorizations); and the B-model of the Fano (given by the derived category of sheaves) is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
            (given by the Fukaya-Seidel category). A few of the relevant names: Kontsevich, Hori-Vafa, Auroux, Katzarkov, Orlov, Seidel, …
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_tropical_geometry Relation to tropical geometry.
          %p
            Close relation to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tropical+geometry"} tropical geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Gross11"} Gross 11
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+map"} mirror map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+Calabi-Yau+spaces"} moduli space of Calabi-Yau spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+mirror+symmetry"} 3d mirror symmetry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+physics"}> duality in physics
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+duality"} geometric Langlands duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The original statement of the homological mirror symmetry conjecture is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homological algebra of mirror symmetry
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/alg-geom/9411018"} alg-geom/9411018
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Aspinwall"}> Paul Aspinwall
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Greene"} Brian Greene
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Morrison"} David Morrison
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Calabi-Yau moduli space, mirror manifolds and spacetime topology change in string theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9309097"} arXiv:hep-th/9309097
            %li
              %p
